        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-a778cda3-f4ce-4bd0-b333-b48cf564d613\",\"url\":\"https://docs.python.org/3.12/whatsnew/3.12.html\",\"title\":\"What’s New In Python 3.12 — Python 3.12.15 documentation\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T03:44:19.482Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 217-255]\\nPEP 693 – Python 3.12 Release Schedule\\n\\n[characters 977-1444]\\nThis article doesn’t attempt to provide a complete specification of all new features, but instead gives a convenient overview. For full details, you should refer to the documentation, such as the Library Reference and Language Reference. If you want to understand the complete implementation and design rationale for a change, refer to the PEP for a particular new feature; but note that PEPs usually are not kept up-to-date once a feature has been fully implemented.\\n\\n[characters 1468-1521]\\nPEP 695, type parameter syntax and the type statement\\n\\n[characters 1546-1579]\\nPEP 701, f-strings in the grammar\\n\\n[characters 1608-1645]\\nPEP 684, a unique per-interpreter GIL\\n\\n[characters 1647-1677]\\nPEP 669, low impact monitoring\\n\\n[characters 1806-1852]\\nPEP 688, using the buffer protocol from Python\\n\\n[characters 2356-2448]\\nDue to the changes in PEP 701, producing tokens via the tokenize module is up to 64% faster.\\n\\n[characters 2740-2768]\\nPEP 697, unstable C API tier\\n\\n[characters 2770-2795]\\nPEP 683, immortal objects\\n\\n[characters 2835-2866]\\nPEP 709, comprehension inlining\\n\\n[characters 2995-3040]\\nPEP 692, using TypedDict to annotate **kwargs\\n\\n[characters 3042-3078]\\nPEP 698, typing.override() decorator\\n\\n[characters 3131-3250]\\nPEP 623: Remove wstr from Unicode objects in Python’s C API, reducing the size of every str object by at least 8 bytes.\\n\\n[characters 3252-3475]\\nPEP 632: Remove the distutils package. See the migration guide for advice replacing the APIs it provided. The third-party Setuptools package continues to provide distutils, if you still require it in Python 3.12 and beyond.\\n\\n[characters 3863-3906]\\nNew Features\\nPEP 695: Type Parameter Syntax\\n\\n[characters 3908-4083]\\nGeneric classes and functions under PEP 484 were declared using a verbose syntax that left the scope of type parameters unclear and required explicit declarations of variance.\\n\\n[characters 4085-4181]\\nPEP 695 introduces a new, more compact and explicit way to create generic classes and functions:\\n\\n[characters 4359-4494]\\nIn addition, the PEP introduces a new way to declare type aliases using the type statement, which creates an instance of TypeAliasType:\\n\\n[characters 5959-5988]\\nSee PEP 695 for more details.\\n\\n[characters 5990-6089]\\n(PEP written by Eric Traut. Implementation by Jelle Zijlstra, Eric Traut, and others in gh-103764.)\\n\\n[characters 6091-6136]\\nPEP 701: Syntactic formalization of f-strings\\n\\n[characters 6138-6450]\\nPEP 701 lifts some restrictions on the usage of f-strings. Expression components inside f-strings can now be any valid Python expression, including strings reusing the same quote as the containing f-string, multi-line expressions, comments, backslashes, and unicode escape sequences. Let’s cover these in detail:\\n\\n[characters 8701-8730]\\nSee PEP 701 for more details.\\n\\n[characters 9632-9838]\\n(Contributed by Pablo Galindo, Batuhan Taskaya, Lysandros Nikolaou, Cristián Maureira-Fredes and Marta Gómez in gh-102856. PEP written by Pablo Galindo, Batuhan Taskaya, Lysandros Nikolaou and Marta Gómez).\\n\\n[characters 9840-9870]\\nPEP 684: A Per-Interpreter GIL\\n\\n[characters 9872-10163]\\nPEP 684 introduces a per-interpreter GIL, so that sub-interpreters may now be created with a unique GIL per interpreter. This allows Python programs to take full advantage of multiple CPU cores. This is currently only available through the C-API, though a Python API is anticipated for 3.13.\\n\\n[characters 10165-10258]\\nUse the new Py_NewInterpreterFromConfig() function to create an interpreter with its own GIL:\\n\\n[characters 10260-10582]\\nPyInterpreterConfig config = {\\n    .check_multi_interp_extensions = 1,\\n    .gil = PyInterpreterConfig_OWN_GIL,\\n};\\nPyThreadState *tstate = NULL;\\nPyStatus status = Py_NewInterpreterFromConfig(&tstate, &config);\\nif (PyStatus_Exception(status)) {\\n    return -1;\\n}\\n/* The new interpreter is now active in the current thread. */\\n\\n[characters 10585-10715]\\nFor further examples how to use the C-API for sub-interpreters with a per-interpreter GIL, see Modules/_xxsubinterpretersmodule.c.\\n\\n[characters 10764-10806]\\nPEP 669: Low impact monitoring for CPython\\n\\n[characters 10808-11151]\\nPEP 669 defines a new API for profilers, debuggers, and other tools to monitor events in CPython. It covers a wide range of events, including calls, returns, lines, exceptions, jumps, and more. This means that you only pay for what you use, providing support for near-zero overhead debuggers and coverage tools. See sys.monitoring for details.\\n\\n[characters 11198-11254]\\nPEP 688: Making the buffer protocol accessible in Python\\n\\n[characters 11256-11404]\\nPEP 688 introduces a way to use the buffer protocol from Python code. Classes that implement the __buffer__() method are now usable as buffer types.\\n\\n[characters 11675-11706]\\nPEP 709: Comprehension inlining\\n\\n[characters 11708-11846]\\nDictionary, list, and set comprehensions are now inlined, rather than creating a new single-use function object for each execution of the \",\"truncated\":true,\"query\":\"per-interpreter GIL PEP 684 subinterpreters threads\",\"excerpts\":[{\"charStart\":217,\"charEnd\":255,\"text\":\"PEP 693 – Python 3.12 Release Schedule\"},{\"charStart\":977,\"charEnd\":1444,\"text\":\"This article doesn’t attempt to provide a complete specification of all new features, but instead gives a convenient overview. For full details, you should refer to the documentation, such as the Library Reference and Language Reference. If you want to understand the complete implementation and design rationale for a change, refer to the PEP for a particular new feature; but note that PEPs usually are not kept up-to-date once a feature has been fully implemented.\"},{\"charStart\":1468,\"charEnd\":1521,\"text\":\"PEP 695, type parameter syntax and the type statement\"},{\"charStart\":1546,\"charEnd\":1579,\"text\":\"PEP 701, f-strings in the grammar\"},{\"charStart\":1608,\"charEnd\":1645,\"text\":\"PEP 684, a unique per-interpreter GIL\"},{\"charStart\":1647,\"charEnd\":1677,\"text\":\"PEP 669, low impact monitoring\"},{\"charStart\":1806,\"charEnd\":1852,\"text\":\"PEP 688, using the buffer protocol from Python\"},{\"charStart\":2356,\"charEnd\":2448,\"text\":\"Due to the changes in PEP 701, producing tokens via the tokenize module is up to 64% faster.\"},{\"charStart\":2740,\"charEnd\":2768,\"text\":\"PEP 697, unstable C API tier\"},{\"charStart\":2770,\"charEnd\":2795,\"text\":\"PEP 683, immortal objects\"},{\"charStart\":2835,\"charEnd\":2866,\"text\":\"PEP 709, comprehension inlining\"},{\"charStart\":2995,\"charEnd\":3040,\"text\":\"PEP 692, using TypedDict to annotate **kwargs\"},{\"charStart\":3042,\"charEnd\":3078,\"text\":\"PEP 698, typing.override() decorator\"},{\"charStart\":3131,\"charEnd\":3250,\"text\":\"PEP 623: Remove wstr from Unicode objects in Python’s C API, reducing the size of every str object by at least 8 bytes.\"},{\"charStart\":3252,\"charEnd\":3475,\"text\":\"PEP 632: Remove the distutils package. See the migration guide for advice replacing the APIs it provided. The third-party Setuptools package continues to provide distutils, if you still require it in Python 3.12 and beyond.\"},{\"charStart\":3863,\"charEnd\":3906,\"text\":\"New Features\\nPEP 695: Type Parameter Syntax\"},{\"charStart\":3908,\"charEnd\":4083,\"text\":\"Generic classes and functions under PEP 484 were declared using a verbose syntax that left the scope of type parameters unclear and required explicit declaration