er with its own GIL:\\n\\n[characters 10260-10582]\\nPyInterpreterConfig config = {\\n    .check_multi_interp_extensions = 1,\\n    .gil = PyInterpreterConfig_OWN_GIL,\\n};\\nPyThreadState *tstate = NULL;\\nPyStatus status = \n\n[... tool result middle pruned ...]\n\nxceptions, jumps, and more. This means that you only pay for what you use, providing support for near-zero overhead debuggers and coverage tools. See sys.monitoring for details.\"},{\"charStart\":11198,\"charEnd\":11254,\"text\":\"PEP 688: Making the buffer protocol accessible in Python\"},{\"charStart\":11256,\"charEnd\":11404,\"text\":\"PEP 688 introduces a way to use the buffer protocol from Python code. Classes that implement the __buffer__() method are now usable as buffer types.\"},{\"charStart\":11675,\"charEnd\":11706,\"text\":\"PEP 709: Comprehension inlining\"},{\"charStart\":11708,\"charEnd\":11846,\"text\":\"Dictionary, list, and set comprehensions are now inlined, rather than creating a new single-use function object for each execution of the \"}],\"sourcePath\":\"C:\\\\Temp\\\\weftmate-fact1-f015661b-84f9-4f3d-a51c-6df83ca18183\\\\profile\\\\conversations\\\\4bafd2bd0f0b22ab80911cf4c69382902c12db0dee367ffc9345785884b7ffd4\\\\session-21a86264-179e-49df-8256-2b8a22dcd268\\\\.weftmate-web-sources\\\\capture-a778cda3-f4ce-4bd0-b333-b48cf564d613.txt\"}"
